# 🔲 LeetCode 75 — Day 22: Equal Row and Column Pairs

**📌 Problem:** [2352. Equal Row and Column Pairs](https://leetcode.com/problems/equal-row-and-column-pairs/)
**🏷️ Topics:** Array, Hash Table, Matrix, Simulation
**⚡ Difficulty:** Medium

---

## 🧠 Intuition

Checking every row against every column directly would mean comparing `n` rows to `n` columns, one element at a time — `O(n³)` in total. Instead, since `vector<int>` can be used as a hash map key, we can count how many times each **row** appears as a whole, then for every **column**, just look up how many rows matched it exactly.

---

## 🛠️ Approach

1. Build a map `mp` where the key is an entire row (`vector<int>`) and the value is how many times that exact row appears in the grid.
2. Loop through every row index and increment `mp[grid[row]]`.
3. For every column index `col`, build a temporary vector `temp` by collecting `grid[r][col]` for every row `r` — this reconstructs the column as a standalone vector.
4. Look up `mp[temp]` — this tells us how many rows exactly match this column. Add that count to the running total.
5. Return `count` after checking all columns.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n²)` — building the row map takes `O(n²)` (n rows, each of length n), and reconstructing + looking up each column also takes `O(n²)` total (n columns, each requiring an `O(n)` build plus an `O(n)` map lookup/hash on a vector key).
- **Space complexity:** `O(n²)` — the map stores up to `n` full rows, each of length `n`.

---

## 💻 Code

**Language/Format:** C++ — solved using LeetCode's OOP submission format (`class Solution` with a public method), using `map<vector<int>, int>` to count occurrences of entire rows as keys.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int equalPairs(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        int n = grid.size();
        int count = 0;

        map<vector<int>, int> mp;

        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            mp[grid[row]]++;
        }

        for (int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
            vector<int> temp;
            for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
                temp.push_back(grid[r][col]);
            }
            count += mp[temp];
        }
        return count;
    }
};
```

---

## 🔑 Key Takeaway

A `vector<int>` can be used directly as a key in `map` (since it supports lexicographic comparison), which makes "compare whole rows/sequences as a single unit" problems much cleaner than manually comparing element by element. Also worth remembering: loop variables are scoped to their own loop — reusing a name like `row` from an earlier loop in a different one won't compile, even if the intent is obvious.

